described as a historic moment g7  leaders pledged their collective might  to a plan to fight future pandemics  the carbus bay declaration aims to  develop vaccines in under 100 days  to build better scientific surveillance  techniques and to beef up  powers for the world health organization  but some were  eager to stress that the current  pandemic is not yet over  we need really those was that the power  to come together  and to organize an effective response  to the covet and the only way to be  effective in response to the covet  is guarantee that everybody will  vaccinate it sooner rather than late  the key thing to remember however is  that the pandemic is raging  right now people need doses right now  and crucially countries need financing  right now and every single announcement  we've seen from this g7 so far comes  without any extra money  and until we see the money none of this  unfortunately can be credible  big promises sometimes empty are often a  feature of big summits  this one though had something to offer  that does feel substantial  the united states have said before we're  back the us is back we feel  very very strongly about the cohesion of  nato and  i for one think that the european union  is an incredibly strong and  vibrant entity joe biden's  administration wants g7 allies to  support  a global infrastructure project to rival  china's belt and road initiative  it's a return to u.s leadership that  many of his fellow leaders have sorely  missed  even if warm words for the eu won't be  entirely to the taste of the summit's  host  boris johnson said at the outset that  this g7 summit would be an opportunity  to reaffirm the values that bind these  seven  developed world democracies and yet at  this very summit  he stands accused by the european union  of failing to honour his own commitments  on brexit in a series of meetings eu  leaders have told  johnson that better relations will only  come if he abides by the terms of  post-brexit trade arrangements in  northern ireland  and the u.s president with irish roots  appears to be on their side  i think that we'll have some pragmatic  solutions and  if we don't then i think that what  our friends have also understood is that  it's the prime duty of the uk  government to uphold the territorial  integrity of the united kingdom which is  the sound of a prime minister no matter  how isolated and however disappointed  that brexit has cast a cloud over his  summit  nevertheless refusing to back down joe  nahul al jazeera cornwall
 

